<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Here Is a Map of the Town.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe changes in places. Interactive: Talk about preferences and familiar topics in a guided class discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and compare changes in local places on a map (e.g., "The market used to be small, but now it is larger."). Can discuss preferences for places in the town square (e.g., "I like La Fondita de Lucia. It has good Panamanian food. What do you think?"). Can ask and answer questions about location preferences (e.g., "Where is the library? Do you like it there?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a simple, large map of a town square (similar to what was used in previous lessons). Students identify familiar places (e.g., "This is the park," "There is a fountain."). Teacher asks, "What places do you see here? Are there any places you like to visit?" Students respond with simple sentences. This activates vocabulary like "park," "fountain," "statue," "market," "library," and prepositions like "near," "next to."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to two locations on the map, one "old" and one "new" (e.g., "The old market" and "The new market" if the map shows changes, or simply two different places). Teacher models asking about preferences: "I like the park. It is quiet. What about you?" And describing location: "The library is across from the town hall." Teacher models pronunciation of /s/ in "street" and stress in "monument," "tradition," encouraging choral repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The market is *next to* the school,' where is the market?" (Checks understanding of 'next to'.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask, 'Do you like the park?' what kind of answer do I expect?" (Checks understanding of preference questions.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated short dialogue between two friends discussing changes in their town square and their favorite places, using a visual "Then and Now" map or two separate maps. The dialogue includes "there is/are," WH-questions, comparatives (e.g., "It used to be small, but now it is larger"), and expressions of preference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally in short exchanges to describe and compare places and express preferences for familiar topics, such as local attractions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue and answer gist questions orally: "What are the friends talking about?" "What place has changed?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False statements about the dialogue (e.g., "The library used to be across from the market. True/False."). Students justify their answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Matching activity: Students match phrases describing the "old" town square with phrases describing the "new" town square based on the dialogue (e.g., "small market" -> "larger market").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using "there is/there are" to describe locations, WH-questions for asking about places ("Where is...?"), expressing preferences, and describing changes in places. Vocabulary: town square, map, market, library, park, statue, fountain, historic, modern, busy, quiet, next to, across from, behind, in front of.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Repetition):</b> The teacher shows images of places in a town square and provides sentence frames. Students complete sentences orally, focusing on "There is/are..." and prepositions. E.g., Teacher shows a park: "There is a _____." (park). Teacher shows a market next to a school: "The market is _____ the school." (next to). The teacher leads choral repetition, emphasizing /s/ clusters in words like "street" and correct stress for "monument."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Question & Answer Chain Drill):</b> In pairs, students use a simplified map showing various places. Student A asks, "Where is the [library]?" Student B answers, "It's [next to] the [park]." Then Student B asks about another place. The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation and grammar. Focus on "Where is...?", "It is...", and prepositions of place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Preferences & Changes):</b> Using two versions of the town square map (e.g., "Old Town Square" and "New Town Square"), students work in pairs. Student A describes a change in one place: "The old market was small, but the new market is larger." Student B asks, "Do you like the new market?" Student A expresses a preference: "Yes, I like the new market. It is more spacious." They swap roles and practice describing other changes and preferences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play / Guided Interview (in small groups of 3-4).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students work in small groups. Each group receives a "mystery map" of a town square (either a fictional map or a simplified local map) with some key places labeled. One student acts as a "tourist" who has just arrived and wants to explore the town square. The other students are "local guides." The tourist asks about places and expresses preferences, and the guides describe locations, explain directions using imperatives and sequencing words (e.g., "First, turn right. Then, walk straight."), and talk about what they like or dislike about different places (e.g., "The museum is historic, and it's near the plaza. I like it because it's quiet."). They should also try to incorporate phrases like "It used to be..." if there are implied changes in the map (e.g., an empty lot becoming a park).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tourist: "Excuse me, where is the library?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guide 1: "The library? It's on Main Street, across from the park."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tourist: "Is it busy or quiet?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guide 2: "It's usually quiet. I like to visit it. What do you think, [Guide 3's name]?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guide 3: "Yes, it’s a good place. Also, to get there, first, walk straight ahead, then turn left at the fountain. The library is on your right."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher provides sentence starters and a checklist of target language to use (e.g., "Ask 3 'Where' questions," "Use 2 prepositions," "Express 1 preference," "Describe 1 change").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group role-play and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their role-play from Stage 4. Each group presents a short segment of their interaction to the class. Peers provide feedback using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /s/ clusters and word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'market,' 'fountain,' 'busy,' 'quiet').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "There is/are" and prepositions of place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and answer WH-questions about location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to express preferences for places.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and clear communication within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I practiced talking about __________ and __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today is __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about describing places or giving directions, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our town square discussion did you enjoy the most?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What speaking strategies worked best for encouraging interaction? Were students able to use "there is/are" and WH-questions accurately in the performance task? Did they confidently express preferences? I observed good use of prepositions, but some students struggled with sequencing words for directions. For next time, I need to provide more explicit practice with "First, then, next" for giving directions in a clear sequence. I will also incorporate more visual cues for comparing "old" and "new" places.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice of describing places and giving directions will prepare students for Lesson 4, where they will write a short descriptive paragraph or set of instructions about their town square, consolidating the vocabulary and grammar used today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>